norm@cfctech.cfc.com (Norman J. Meluch) writes:

> I think of "inactivation" as the mimic hiding after you disturb it.

> Actually, the code is more humorous than that. The check to hide the
> mimic is not a proximity check, but a "vision" check. As soon as the
> 'hero' cannot *see* a mimic, the mimic "inactivates". Thus there is
> the ostrich method of dealing with mimics in a shop. If the mimic has
> been disturbed, just put on the blindfold! That way the mimic is
> 'unseen' and hides once more.

> Cute eh? Just cover your eyes, and the problem goes away!


